// Generated by scripts/restructure-by-construct.
// Per-construct DSL layout: every <kind>s.memql file in a domain
// holds every construct of that kind in the domain. The companion
// files cover the other construct kinds.

// A POLICY SAYS WHERE TO LOOK; A RULE SAYS WHICH CALLS IT IS FOR.
// The rules are in dsl/rules/rules.memql. Nothing in this file decides which
// calls it serves, and that separation is why there are three policies here
// rather than the six a call-site-names-its-own-policy world needed.
//
// THE ORDER INSIDE A CHAIN IS THREE KINDS OF COST, INCREASING:
//
//   fleet:      a model on hardware the person already owns. Costs electricity.
//   app:        a signed-in Claude Code or Codex on one of their machines.
//               Costs a subscription they already pay for; MemQL is not billed.
//   federation: a metered vendor. Costs money, and is therefore the hop the
//               cost ceiling gates -- and gates only when a local door preceded
//               it, because a chain that STARTS at a vendor is a decision
//               somebody made rather than a fallback.
//
// `fleet:strongest` AND `app:*` ARE SELECTORS, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THIS
// SHIPPABLE AS A DEFAULT. A policy naming one model id would park on every
// fleet running something else, and which weights a person pulled is not
// knowable here. A selector resolves per call, among the models that can serve
// THAT call. `fleet:*` was the old spelling of `fleet:strongest` and is
// retired: it said "any", which is not what it did.
//
// A POLICY NOTHING CAN NAME IS A DECORATION. That rule cost this file four
// policies once already and six more in this epic. `localOnly` is the one
// exemption and it is deliberate: no shipped rule names it, because it exists
// as the VOCABULARY a custom rule uses -- an owner saying "this work does not
// leave my hardware" needs a policy to point at, and inventing one from the OS
// is a worse first experience than finding one. Delete it only if the rule
// grammar stops being able to name it.

/// The default chain: local strongest, then a signed-in app on the person's own
/// machine, then the cheapest federated model that qualifies. Named by every
/// shipped rule except reasoningParks.
///
/// `federation:cheapest` orders by input plus output cost per million ascending
/// among the records that can serve the call. A vendor record with no cost
/// figures sorts LAST and the decision record says so -- sorting it first would
/// make a price nobody filled in the cheapest thing in the catalog.
@primary("fleet:strongest")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("federation:cheapest")
policy localFirst { }

/// Local only, with no fallback authored on purpose. When the fleet cannot
/// serve the call the rule's onUnavailable decides, and every door being shut
/// is the honest answer rather than a vendor call the person did not ask for.
///
/// No shipped rule names this. It is the policy a CUSTOM rule points at, which
/// is why it survives the no-decorations rule this file's header states.
@primary("fleet:strongest")
policy localOnly { }

/// Local strongest, then an app, then the STRONGEST federated model rather than
/// the cheapest. Named by reasoningParks, for work where a wrong answer costs
/// more than a dear one.
///
/// It is still paid-last: the vendor hop is third and the cost ceiling gates
/// it exactly as it gates localFirst's. What differs is which vendor record
/// wins once the chain gets there.
@primary("fleet:strongest")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("federation:strongest")
policy federationStrongest { }
